and then they go on feeling entitled,

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:39.290
then when they do do something like that

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.370
and they do something wrong,

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:42.700
put someone in a situation,

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:43.940
they don't even know they've done it,

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:44.920
and they're so blase.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:47.700
It can literally, as you
say, ruin someone's life.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:48.710
And it can be stopped--
- I think that's why

00:29:48.710 --> 00:29:51.990
language is so important,
like I think that thing about,

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.220
what you said when you were like,

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.490
oh I've been sexually assaulted.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:57.250
I found it, even when I
was writing the script,

00:29:57.250 --> 00:29:58.800
like, there was a moment where,

00:29:59.697 --> 00:30:00.610
I can't even actually remember

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:01.470
if it ended up in the episode,

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:02.390
it might have been cut out,

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:05.570
but there was a moment
where Aimee says, like,

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:06.900
I've been sexually assaulted.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:09.230
And even as I wrote it, there
was a little thing in my head

00:30:09.230 --> 00:30:11.070
where I was like, oh, like, has she?

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:12.850
You know, cause it feels so heavy.

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:13.683
- [Laura] Yeah.

00:30:13.683 --> 00:30:14.950
- And then you're like, yes.
- Yeah.

00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:16.940
- That is exactly what it is.

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:19.350
And like, that's the language
that needs to be used.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:22.700
Not just, oh, he was
a bit of a creepy guy,

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:23.900
or that was a bit of a--
- Absolutely,

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:24.740
or groping.
- Yes.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:26.250
- That's the euphemism we used, right?

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.972
We say groping, cause we don't
like to say sexual assault.

00:30:28.972 --> 00:30:29.805
- Yeah.

00:30:29.805 --> 00:30:32.000
- Because people tell us
it's not that big a deal.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:32.833
- Yes.

00:30:32.833 --> 00:30:34.460
- I think for a lot of girls,
if they went to their friends

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:36.380
and said, I've been sexually assaulted,

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:38.250
and then described that scenario,

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:40.620
like friends, even, would say,

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:42.230
why were you being so melodramatic?

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:43.540
That's not a sexual assault.

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:44.380
Because we don't know,

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:46.260
we grow up thinking oh,
this is just normal.

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:48.910
And it's normal cause it
starts from such a young age.

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:52.360
We know that nearly,
just under one in three

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:55.310
16 to 18 year old girls experience

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:57.660
unwanted sexual touching at school.

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.770
In other words, about a
third of teenage girls

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:02.180
are sexually assaulted at school.

00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:05.030
And if it starts then, then
where do you go from there?

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:06.948
If it's happened at school
where you should be safe--

00:31:06.948 --> 00:31:07.930
- It's not good.

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:10.780
- It's just so much more
common than we'd like to think

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:11.740
from such a young age,

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.120
and if we don't talk about
it cause we're uncomfortable,

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:15.900
then we don't give
those girls the language

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:17.540
to name what's happening to them,

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:21.050
we don't give them the power
to know that they can object,

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:23.410
and like you said, we
don't teach boys to know

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:25.520
what their rights and responsibilities are

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:27.120
in navigating relationships either.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:28.529
So everyone's confused.

00:31:28.529 --> 00:31:29.362
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:31:29.362 --> 00:31:30.540
- And it just doesn't help anyone.

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:32.200
- I think when you realize

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:34.850
that it's like every woman that you know,

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:37.883
it's almost like a, sort of,

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.030
like you're breaking out of

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.287
some kind of psychosis or something,

00:31:42.287 --> 00:31:43.686
where you're like, oh!

00:31:43.686 --> 00:31:44.736
- You're not alone.

00:31:44.736 --> 00:31:45.569
- Yeah, it's not just me.
- We're all just

00:31:45.569 --> 00:31:47.165
living with this.

00:31:47.165 --> 00:31:48.420
- We haven't talked about it.
- And we're not really talking

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:49.710
about it, or saying anything.

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.920
- And it's so important, I
think, that this storyline

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:55.360
really looks at the
long-term impact that it has.

00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:57.960
Because so often, these
incidents are dismissed,

00:31:57.960 --> 00:31:58.820
it's not a big deal,

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:00.330
like surely that's not that big a deal?

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:02.560
It was just a one off,
whatever people say,

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:05.280
and the truth is, just
like Aimee discovers,

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:06.830
it affects your whole life.

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:09.410
It affects how you feel,
how you feel about yourself,

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:12.360
how you feel in crowds,
amongst other people,

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:13.360
where you can go.
- Relationships.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:15.110
- How you can get there,
your relationships.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:16.413
(strident rhythmic music)

00:32:16.413 --> 00:32:17.713
- What are you doing here?

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:21.260
- Getting the bus.

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:25.200
We're all getting the bus.

00:32:44.253 --> 00:32:45.660
- [Bus Driver] Are you
getting on, or what?

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:46.660
- Give her a minute.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:50.253
It's just a stupid bus.

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:53.360
- It's just a stupid bus.

00:32:56.203 --> 00:32:58.490
- And we've just dismissed these stories,

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:00.290
and ignored them for so long,

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:03.730
and finally I think, women
and girls are speaking out,

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:06.610
and it's the power of
that collective voice.

00:33:06.610 --> 00:33:08.730
One of us speaking out on our own,

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:11.540
we get shot down, we get
ignored, we get dismissed,

00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:13.930
but when a whole group
of us stand together,

00:33:13.930 --> 00:33:16.320
as Aimee's friends stand alongside her,

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:17.440
and say, this isn't okay,

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:19.280
we're not taking this shit anymore,

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:20.530
this has happened.

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:22.840
It forces people to
stop and listen to them,

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:25.915
and hopefully it forces us
to change the conversation,

00:33:25.915 --> 00:33:28.010
and to really confront the idea

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:29.640
that this shouldn't just be normal.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:30.510
- Yeah.
- It should be,

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:32.210
we've all been there, let's talk about it,

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:33.450
this needs to fucking change.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:35.358
Because we shouldn't have all been there.

